"""
Bayes Factor for Normal Means — is this effect real? Bayesian style.

WHAT IT DOES: Given data assumed N(θ, 1), compares H1 (θ ≠ 0, with prior θ ~ N(0, τ²))
against H0 (θ = 0). Returns the Bayes factor: BF > 1 means the data favor an effect;
BF < 1 favors none; magnitude = strength of evidence. Uses conjugate priors and
closed-form marginal likelihoods.

WHEN TO USE: You have normally distributed data with known variance (σ²=1) and want
a Bayesian answer to "is there an effect?" The Bayes factor tells you how many times
more likely the data are under H1 vs H0 — a continuous measure of evidence.

GUARANTEES: BF > 1 when data comes from H1 (θ ≠ 0). BF < 1 when data comes from H0
(θ = 0). Both directions asserted (two-sided test).

⚠️ WRONG MATHEMATICS as shipped (AURA-E010). The evidence term is missing n·τ² in the
numerator. Consequence: 30 points at θ=2.0 give BF=1.24 (should be 3×10²⁴). The
planted demo passes by a hair because the test is too easy. See the retrofit for the
correct formula, verified against brute-force numeric integration.
"""
